<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Noin-Ula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE–1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Noyon Uul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Noin-Ula (Noyon Uul) is a group of elite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> burial mounds in the hills north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Ulaanbaatar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. First excavated by Pyotr Kozlov's expedition in 1924–1925, its tombs preserved woollen carpets, embroidered textiles, Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">lacquerware</a>, which are among the best evidence for Xiongnu elite culture and its links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and the Hellenistic world.</p></div>
